[{:seon.task/name "web-server-threads-are-joined-at-stop"
  :seon.task/plan "When the web proc binds its port, ring-chez-adapter's :fibers strategy runs its acceptor and its connection sweeper as two futures (two OS threads of Jolt's agent pool, clojure-agent-pool; the adapter's `start-sweeper!` and its acceptors in src/ring_chez/adapter.clj). The acceptor polls accept() in accept-poll-ms slices; the sweeper is (Thread/sleep 100) in a loop, a clock (AGENTS §4). stop-server waits for the acceptor but only flags the sweeper, so after `seon.root/stop!` the sweeper outlives its owner for up to 100 ms: root's thread count after stop! depends on timing, so bin/check lifecycle runs with SEON_WEB_PORT=0. The adapter's :keep-alive-timeout-ms 600000 and :write-timeout-ms 60000 (`seon.web/web-proc`) are application timers and literals (docs/clocks.md#time-decides-owed-its-owners-fix). Correct: the listener is a fiber parked on the listen fd's readiness (jolt.io-poller); an idle connection parks on its own read (a fact, not a sweep); a stuck write is the peer's half-open socket, the kernel's keepalive (the owner's exception); the web proc's ::flow/stop returns after both are gone. Then `seon.check/declared-threads` drops its \"clojure-agent-pool\" 2 for a listening web proc, and lifecycle runs with the web on. Deletes: start-sweeper!, the accept poll slice, the two adapter timeouts, the SEON_WEB_PORT=0 in bin/check's lifecycle step. A dependency change: on a jolt/<topic> branch of the adapter. Read 2026-11-06: `seon.web/web-proc` still passes the two timeouts, and `seon.check/declared-threads` still declares \"clojure-agent-pool\" 2. The proof named below is green on main with that declaration, so it shows the change only once that line goes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.root-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tabs-first-keyframe-settles-on-its-own"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-08 in bin/check idle (a land of jolt/rootagent with main's af549ba258 and f9bda5bd88 merged): the check's tab opened the root view's feed and waited about 30 minutes for its \": settled\" mark; the check's root was idle (every fiber parked on core.async alts, no CPU). A second request to the same feed (curl /feed?view=root) got its keyframe at once, and within seconds the check's own tab settled and the step ended IDLE OK. So a tab's first keyframe or settled mark can wait on another tab opening the same view: the web proc's open of a tab (seon.web http-in :open: :views to the render proc, then :keyframe) or the render proc's answer to it loses a keyframe asked while another is in flight, or answers only a view's newest tab. Evidence: the Web session's scratchpad idle-hang-40858 (fiber dump, sample, lsof) and the run's target/check output. Find the cause before fixing. Read so far (Web, 2026-11-08): a tab is stale until a keyframe reaches it (seon.web keyframe!, to every stale tab of its view), and a settled mark goes only to fresh tabs (offer-all skips stale ones), so a tab whose keyframe never comes never settles: the second tab's keyframe reached the check's stale tab too, which is why it unstuck. The render proc answers a keyframe at once when the view is ready? (rendered; a branch read), else holds it in :kf-wanted and asks root's state, sending it from the pass an answer brings; a pass runs only on an input, so a wanted keyframe whose answer's pass still finds its view not ready, with nothing else in flight, waits for ever. Ruled out: a nil keyframe (view-html composes a string skeleton; compose refuses nil by contract). Next: the pass after :state's answer for the root view (root-page is nil until main is read: is main's read asked, and does its answer's pass send :kf-wanted?), and keyframe! closing a tab whose offer! is refused.

Proofs, red first: a tab that opens a view while that view's keyframe for an earlier tab is in flight gets its own keyframe and settled mark, with no other tab opening."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.web-test/each-tab-gets-its-own-keyframe}}]
